      "slot_id": "r10",
      "parent_slot_id": "r9",
      "depth": 1,
      "role": "signal",
      "persona": "the_constexpr_oracle",
      "body": "Good question, and a common one. `std::async` hands you a `future`, but *nothing owns the concurrency*: the future's destructor either blocks (sometimes) or detaches, and cancellation is whatever you bolt on by hand. Structured concurrency ties each task's lifetime to a lexical scope — when the scope exits, every child has either completed or been cancelled, guaranteed, before the next line runs. The proposal's `task<T>` is that scope-bound owner. So it's less \"a faster async\" and more \"async with the lifetimes made explicit and enforced.\"",
      "tag": null,
      "is_op": false,
      "is_mod": false,
      "controversial": false,
      "edited": false,
      "deleted": false,
      "removed": false,
      "collapsed": false,
